---
layout: article
title: Encoding profiles
description: Learn how Appwrite Videos encoding profiles set the resolution, bitrate, and codec of each rendition, and how profile changes apply.
---

An **encoding profile** is a reusable encoding target. It sets the resolution, video bitrate, audio bitrate, and codec for every [rendition](/docs/products/videos/renditions) encoded from it. Profiles belong to a project and are shared by all its videos.

Together, the profiles you encode a video with form its **bitrate ladder**: a set of renditions from low to high quality that players switch between as bandwidth changes.

# Resolution and bitrate {% #resolution-and-bitrate %}

Two numbers decide how a rendition looks and how much data it needs:

- **Resolution** is the picture size, such as 1280 × 720. Larger pictures show more detail on large screens, but a phone in a pocket-sized player gains little from 4K.
- **Bitrate** is how much data each second of video uses. More bits mean fewer compression artifacts, but the viewer's connection must keep up. A 5,000 kbps rendition needs a steady connection of about 5 Mbps or more.

A good profile pairs the two: enough bitrate for its resolution to look clean, and no more. Fast motion, like sports or games, needs more bitrate than a talking head at the same resolution.

# Start with no profiles {% #start-with-no-profiles %}

New projects have no profiles. Create the ones you need before you encode your first video. The [presets](#presets) are a good starting point.

# What a profile contains {% #what-a-profile-contains %}

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `name` | Label shown in the Console, for example `720p` |
| `width`, `height` | Target picture size in pixels |
| `videoBitRate` | Target video bitrate in **kilobits per second** |
| `audioBitRate` | Target audio bitrate in **kilobits per second** |
| `codec` | Video codec. Defaults to `h264` (H.264). List the codecs you can use with `videos.listCodecs()` |

Width and height can be from 16 to 4,320 pixels. Video bitrate can be from 32 to 20,000 kbps, and audio bitrate from 32 to 512 kbps. Each profile must be unique: two profiles cannot share the same width, height, bitrates, and codec.

# Presets {% #presets %}

When you create a profile in the Console, you can start from a preset or enter your own values under **Custom profile**:

| Preset | Resolution | Video bitrate | Audio bitrate |
|--------|------------|---------------|---------------|
| 360p | 640 × 360 | 800 kbps | 96 kbps |
| 480p | 854 × 480 | 1,400 kbps | 128 kbps |
| 720p | 1280 × 720 | 2,800 kbps | 128 kbps |
| 1080p | 1920 × 1080 | 5,000 kbps | 192 kbps |
| 1440p | 2560 × 1440 | 8,000 kbps | 192 kbps |
| 2160p | 3840 × 2160 | 16,000 kbps | 192 kbps |

# Create a profile {% #create-a-profile %}

1. Open **Videos** in your project and select **Encoding profiles**.
2. Click **Create profile**.
3. Choose a preset, or switch to **Custom profile** and enter a **Name**, **Width**, **Height**, **Video bitrate**, and **Audio bitrate**.
4. Click **Create**.

The new profile shows up in the **Profiles** list when you create a video or add renditions. Profile names are labels only, so name them by purpose if that helps your team, for example `720p-mobile`.

With a server SDK, create profiles from the `project` service:

{% info title="Required scope" %}
The API key used for these calls needs the `project.profiles.write` scope.
{% /info %}

{% multicode %}
  ```server-nodejs
  import { Client, Project, VideoCodec } from 'node-appwrite';

  const client = new Client()
      .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
      .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
      .setKey('<API_KEY>');

  const project = new Project(client);

  const profile = await project.createVideoProfile({
      name: '720p',
      width: 1280,
      height: 720,
      videoBitRate: 2800,
      audioBitRate: 128,
      codec: VideoCodec.H264 // optional, defaults to H.264
  });
  ```

  ```http
  POST /v1/project/videos/profiles HTTP/1.1
  Content-Type: application/json
  X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
  X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

  {
    "name": "720p",
    "width": 1280,
    "height": 720,
    "videoBitRate": 2800,
    "audioBitRate": 128
  }
  ```
{% /multicode %}

List profiles with `project.listVideoProfiles()`. Results are sorted from the lowest resolution to the highest, and you can search them by name with `Query.search('search', '720p')`.

# Design a ladder {% #design-a-ladder %}

You do not need many profiles. Three to five usually cover every viewer, from a weak mobile connection to a fast desktop one. Each extra rendition adds encoding time and storage.

| Use case | Suggested profiles |
|----------|--------------------|
| Short social clips, mostly on phones | 360p, 480p, 720p |
| General web and app video | 360p, 480p, 720p, 1080p |
| High-detail content on large screens | 480p, 720p, 1080p, 1440p, 2160p |
| Audio-only, such as podcasts | One profile. Only its audio bitrate matters |

When you build your own ladder:

- **Start low enough.** The lowest rendition is what viewers on a poor connection get. Without one around 360p, they buffer instead of playing.
- **Space the rungs out.** Each step should roughly double the bitrate of the one below. Rungs that are too close waste encoding time, since players gain little by switching between them.
- **Stop at the source.** A rung above the source resolution only upscales. See [Upscaling](#upscaling).
- **Keep audio steady.** Viewers notice audio quality changing more than picture quality. Use the same audio bitrate across most rungs, as the presets do.

# Upscaling {% #upscaling %}

A profile that is larger than the source picture **upscales** it. Upscaling adds size and encoding time without adding detail. The Console marks these profiles as **Upscale** and leaves them out of the default selection when you create renditions.

The check compares short edge with short edge and long edge with long edge, so it works the same for landscape and portrait video. A 1080 × 1920 portrait clip does not upscale with the `1080p` profile (1920 × 1080), because both edges match. Until the source is probed, its size is unknown and no profile is marked.

You can still encode an upscaling profile, for example to keep every video on the same ladder. It just costs more without looking better.

# Changes and deletion {% #changes-and-deletion %}

Profiles are configuration, not media:

- **Updating** a profile does not re-encode renditions that already exist. To apply the change, delete those renditions and create them again. An update replaces every field except the codec, so pass the name, size, and bitrates each time. To change the codec, create a new profile.
- **Deleting** a profile keeps the renditions that were already encoded with it. Each rendition keeps its own copy of the profile's size and bitrates.
